Fixed #1114: the last two flag readers that walked raw argv now skip value slots. Committed as 6b9f6e52, and fmt, clippy (-D warnings), build and the full cargo test all passed.

The bug: a flag-shaped word that was really another flag's value still got read as a flag by these two helpers. I found a third reader with the same problem: numeric_flag_refusal, which refuses bad numbers at startup.

| command | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| yoyo --system --max-turns -p hi | refused, exit 2 (--max-turns value '-p' is not a number) | runs; --max-turns is just the system prompt |
| yoyo --system --deny -p hi | -p added as a deny rule | no deny rule |
| yoyo --system --max-turns 3 | max_turns = 3 | not applied |
| yoyo --max-turns -p hi (a real flag) | refused | still refused |

The change:
• One shared walk. New cli_config::option_positions finds every place a flag is written as an option. The existing option_position now uses it, so there's still only one walk.
• All three readers use it: collect_repeatable_flag, resolve_numeric_setting and numeric_flag_refusal.
• Repeated flags still work. --deny a --deny b still collects both.
• Flag-looking values keep today's behaviour. A real --deny whose value looks like a flag still gets that value and the warning. The word it swallowed is no longer also read as a second --deny.
• Callers. I checked all 10 collect_repeatable_flag callers and the numeric ones. Each passes argv with the binary at index 0. The table is in the commit body.

Tests: I added two parse-result tests to src/cli_flag_slot_tests.rs. Both failed before the fix. As a check, I put the old raw walk back into each helper in turn, one at a time, with a NEUTERED marker. Each time the matching test failed by name, and I restored the code in the same command.

Size gate: src/cli.rs went from 7863 to 7861 lines, and I updated its entry in tests/module_size.rs.

Left as is: some older unit tests in cli.rs (around lines 4611–4837) still contain their own copy of the old walk inline. They test that copy, not the real code, so they don't affect users, but they would never catch a regression in it. I didn't touch them because they're outside this task. Since this commit closes #1114, they'd need a new ticket if we want them fixed.
